<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Olmecs</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1200–400 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a> · Also known as Olmec civilisation</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Olmecs were the earliest major civilisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, flourishing in the Gulf Coast lowlands of southern Veracruz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabasco">Tabasco</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> from about 1200 to 400 BCE. Their centres, San Lorenzo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-venta">La Venta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tres-zapotes">Tres Zapotes</a>, are known for colossal stone heads, jade carvings and early pyramids. Their art, religious imagery and ideas of rulership influenced later Mesoamerican cultures, including the Maya.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/olmecs.jpg" alt="Olmecs" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Madman2001 · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olmec_Heartland_Overview_4.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Olmecs comes from the Nahuatl Ōlmēcatl, meaning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> people, from ōlli, natural rubber. The Olmec heartland is a swampy lowland on the Gulf coast, broken by low hills, ridges and volcanoes, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuxtla-mountains">Sierra de los Tuxtlas</a> rising in the north. Pre-Olmec cultures had flourished there since about 2500 BCE, and the Olmecs may have derived in part from the neighbouring Mokaya or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixe">Mixe</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoque">Zoque</a> cultures. They seem to have had their roots in early farming cultures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabasco">Tabasco</a>, which began between 5100 and 4600 BCE. Ritual deposits at the shrine of El Manatí show activity at least as early as 1600 to 1500 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first Olmec centre, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-lorenzo-tenochtitlan">San Lorenzo Tenochtitlán</a>, was almost abandoned around 900 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-venta">La Venta</a> rose to prominence. Many San Lorenzo monuments were destroyed around the 950s BCE, which may point to an internal uprising or, less likely, an invasion. The latest view is that rivers changing course may have been responsible. Other centres included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tres-zapotes">Tres Zapotes</a> and Laguna de los Cerros.</p><p>By about 400 BCE the major centres had been abandoned, and between 400 and 350 BCE the population of the eastern half of the heartland dropped sharply. Scholars have not determined why. Archaeologists think serious environmental changes in the river system, on which farming, hunting and transport depended, probably made the region unsuited to large farming populations. Elements of Olmec tradition lived on in successor societies.</p><p>Olmec culture was unknown to historians until the mid-19th century. In 1869 José Melgar y Serrano published a description of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmec-colossal-heads">colossal head</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tres-zapotes">Tres Zapotes</a>, the first Olmec monument found in place.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Olmec art is the hallmark of the culture, worked in jade, clay, basalt and greenstone. Seventeen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmec-colossal-heads">colossal heads</a> have been unearthed, ranging from 3.4 metres high to 1.47 metres, and the largest weigh between 25 and 55 tonnes. No two are alike, and they are generally accepted as portraits of rulers, perhaps dressed as ballplayers. Jade was a mark of rank among the ruling classes.</p><p>Little is directly known of Olmec political structure. Site surveys show considerable centralisation, first at San Lorenzo and then at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-venta">La Venta</a>. San Lorenzo and La Venta were largely ceremonial centres; most Olmecs lived in villages on higher ground, in houses with storage pits and gardens. Obsidian, which the heartland lacks, came mostly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, and greenstone and marine shell also travelled far.</p><p>The Olmecs are credited, sometimes speculatively, with early bloodletting, the Mesoamerican ballgame and perhaps writing, as in the Cascajal Block, whose status as writing some scholars doubt. Their ethnic and linguistic affiliation is unknown; one proposal, by Lyle Campbell and Terrence Kaufman, is that they spoke a language ancestral to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixe">Mixe</a>–Zoquean.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Olmecs” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Olmecs</b>, who flourished on the Gulf Coast of Mexico from about 1200 to 400 BCE at centres such as San Lorenzo and La Venta, created the first great religious art of the region: colossal stone heads, probably of rulers, jade figures, and images of supernatural beings combining…</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesoamerican-religion">Mesoamerican religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Preclassic they shared symbols and ideas with the <b>Olmecs</b> of the Gulf Coast and other neighbours.</blockquote><a href="/culture/maya-civilisation">Maya civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Olmec</b> heartland lost its primacy, but La Venta's art, ritual and ideas of kingship passed into Epi-Olmec, Maya and other later traditions.</blockquote><a href="/state/la-venta">La Venta</a></li><li><blockquote>Tres Zapotes grew on the western edge of the Tuxtla Mountains in southern Veracruz around 900 BCE, during the <b>Olmec</b> period.</blockquote><a href="/state/tres-zapotes">Tres Zapotes</a></li></ul></section>
